# THE SIBLING OF test_nilpy_import_sqlite.npy, AND THE HALF THAT WAS BROKEN.
#
# Both go through the same door: `import <name>` resolves a C header out of
# /usr/include and links the library dynamically. sqlite3 worked and zlib did
# not, for one reason -- the compiler derives the soname from the header's file
# NAME. `sqlite3.h` -> `libsqlite3.so` happens to be right; `zlib.h` ->
# `libzlib.so` is not, the real library being libz.so.1. So `import zlib` was
# a hard COMPILE error ("this build would die at exec: `crc32` is imported from
# libzlib.so, which no library on this machine answers to") while its sibling
# next door passed, which is why the derivation survived: the first case anyone
# tried was the one where the stem is the library name.
#
# compressBound is the right call to assert on. It is a pure function of its
# argument, so unlike sqlite3_libversion_number() there is no host version to
# accidentally pin -- that mistake is recorded on the row next door
# (bug-n-nilpy-import-sqlite-asserts-host-sqlite-version). zlib's own formula is
# len + (len>>12) + (len>>14) + (len>>25) + 13, so 1000 -> 1013 on every zlib
# ever shipped.
# THE IMPORT IS SPELLED WITH THE HEADER'S EXTENSION, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT
# OF THIS ROW NOW. On 2026-09-11 (80d71d782) `zlib` was added to
# PyRtlUnitServesPython, so a BARE `import zlib` correctly reaches
# lib/rtl/zlib.pas -- our own pure-Pascal inflate plus the Python checksum
# surface -- and no longer reaches /usr/include/zlib.h at all. That is the
# resolver deciding, not breaking: a Python program writing `import zlib` means
# Python's zlib, and a same-named header is an accident of this machine's include
# path.
#
# So this row asks for the C door BY NAME. An explicit extension pins the
# language, which is the documented way to say "I mean the header".
#
# THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE SAID "--no-shims cannot help here: the door that
# closed is the LIST door, not the shim door, and no flag reopens it." That was
# true when written and is false now: the flag lifts BOTH ours-first
# substitutions as of this change, so a bare `import zlib --no-shims` reaches
# the header too. It is kept as the record because it is exactly the claim the
# new sibling exists to pin -- an untested door is how the list door went inert
# under this row in the first place.
#
# THREE SIBLINGS, one per door, and they are each other's controls:
#   this file                          explicit extension -> the header, libz.so.1
#   test_nilpy_import_zlib_ours.npy    bare name          -> lib/rtl/zlib.pas, NO DT_NEEDED
#   test_nilpy_import_zlib_noshims.npy bare name + flag   -> the header, libz.so.1
# The soname derivation this row was written for is exercised by two of the
# three now; the middle one must carry no dynamic dependency at all.
import 'zlib.h' as zlib
print(compressBound(1000))
